            <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 mb-3">4. Nursing Bottle Technique: Avoiding Aspiration Pneumonia</h2>
            <p>
              Aspiration pneumonia—where milk accidentally enters the lungs instead of the stomach—is the #1 cause of death during bottle feeding. Follow this life-saving protocol:
            </p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mt-3">
              <div class="p-4 bg-emerald-50 rounded-2xl border border-emerald-200">
                <div class="font-black text-emerald-950 text-xs uppercase mb-1">✅ Correct Posture (On the Belly)</div>
                <p class="text-xs text-emerald-900 leading-relaxed">
                  Always place the kitten flat on its stomach with paws resting on a towel, mimicking how it nurses from a mother cat. Angle the nursing bottle upward at a 45-degree angle so no air enters the nipple. Let the kitten suckle at its own pace.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="p-4 bg-red-50 rounded-2xl border border-red-200">
                <div class="font-black text-red-950 text-xs uppercase mb-1">❌ Deadly Mistake (On Its Back)</div>
                <p class="text-xs text-red-900 leading-relaxed">
                  NEVER hold a kitten on its back like a human infant. In this position, milk directly trickles into the trachea and floods the bronchial airways, leading to suffocating drowning or fatal bacterial aspiration pneumonia.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 mb-3">5. Post-Feed Burping &amp; Genital Stimulation</h2>
            <p>
              Newborn kittens up to 3 to 4 weeks of age <strong>cannot urinate or defecate autonomously</strong>. Their mother stimulates their bladder and bowels by constantly licking their perineum. As a surrogate caregiver, you must perform this function after every single feeding:
            </p>
            <ol class="space-y-3 text-xs text-slate-700 list-decimal list-inside mt-3">
              <li><strong>Burping:</strong> Hold the kitten upright against your chest or over your shoulder and gently pat its back with one finger until you feel a tiny bubble release.</li>
              <li><strong>Gentle Warm Stimulation:</strong> Take a warm, damp cotton wool pad or tissue and gently massage the urinary opening and anus in circular motions. The kitten will immediately void pale clear-yellow urine and soft mustard-colored stool.</li>
              <li><strong>Clean &amp; Dry:</strong> Gently dab the area dry to avoid urine scald or chilling.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 mb-3">6. Weaning from KMR to Solid Food (Weeks 4–8)</h2>
            <p>
              Around 4 weeks of age, tiny incisor teeth begin breaking through the gums. Start weaning by offering a warm &quot;gruel&quot; in a shallow saucer consisting of 50% warm KMR liquid blended with 50% <strong>Royal Canin Mother &amp; Babycat Mousse</strong>. Once comfortable lapping from a dish, gradually reduce the milk over 2 weeks until the kitten eats pure wet food and soaked dry kibbles.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-3">
